    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the salt is just a random string, does it matter how long it is? and does it need to be kept secret like the password itself or can it just be stored right next to the hash in the database?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The length of the salt is important, though not as critical as making sure it's truly random and unique for each user. A longer salt makes brute-force attacks against the hash significantly more computationally expensive, which is always a good thing. You don't need to keep the salt secret; in fact, you *must* store it alongside the hash in your database. It's designed to be public knowledge alongside the hash so you can correctly verify the password during login.",
