    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get that. so if the salt isn't a secret, does that mean an attacker who gets access to the database can just use the salts to build a new rainbow table specifically for that one user? or does the salt make that process too much work to bother with?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If an attacker gets the database, they have both the hashes *and* the salts, which is exactly what you want them to have for verification purposes. However, the salt completely neutralizes the efficiency of a traditional rainbow table attack. A rainbow table relies on pre-calculating hashes for *millions* of common passwords against a *single* hash function setup. Because each user has a unique salt, the attacker would have to generate a completely separate, unique rainbow table for *every single user* in your database, which is computationally infeasible.",
      "source": "target",
